    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class="radio-actions">
    <a class="radio-btn" href="https://radio.jaikhyapaparampara.com/public/guru_vani_radio/playlist.m3u" rel="noopener">M3U playlist</a>
    <a class="radio-btn" href="https://radio.jaikhyapaparampara.com/public/guru_vani_radio/playlist.pls" rel="noopener">PLS playlist</a>
    <a class="radio-btn" href="/articles/">Read the articles</a>
    <a class="radio-btn" href="/radio/developers/">Developer notes</a>
  </div>
  <p class="radio-note">Streams 24 hours from the Garbhagriha.</p>
  <p class="radio-note"><a href="#radio-listen-heading">How to listen on phones, computers and smart speakers &darr;</a></p>

  <section class="radio-section" aria-labelledby="radio-recent-heading">
    <h2 id="radio-recent-heading">Just played</h2>
    <ul class="radio-recent-list" id="radio-recent-list">
      <li class="radio-recent-empty">Loading the recent set&hellip;</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="radio-section" aria-labelledby="radio-about-heading">
    <h2 id="radio-about-heading">About the station</h2>
    <p>
      Guru Vani Radio is a single continuous stream drawn from the Khyapa
      Parampara archive &mdash; at present the recorded talks of Shri Praveen
      Radhakrishnan. The playlist is ordered chronologically, so a listener who
      leaves the station running hears the teaching as it was actually given,
      one talk following the next, oldest to newest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing is gated. There is no account, no subscription, and no advertising
      &mdash; the same principle that keeps every article and ebook on this site
      free. If a talk moves you, the written archive is where it is recorded in
      full.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="radio-section" aria-labelledby="radio-availability-heading">
    <h2 id="radio-availability-heading">Availability</h2>
    <p>
      Guru Vani Radio is offered free of charge and is a best-effort
      arrangement, not a guarantee: if the station falls silent, it is normally
      brought back within a couple of hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      If it stays quiet for longer than that, or anything else seems wrong,
      write to <a href="mailto:kaliputraashish@gmail.com">kaliputraashish@gmail.com</a>
      and it will be looked at.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="radio-section" aria-labelledby="radio-listen-heading">
    <h2 id="radio-listen-heading">How to listen</h2>
    <p class="listen-intro">
      Guru Vani Radio is free, needs no account, and sounds the same on every
      device. Use whatever you already have &mdash; nothing needs to be
      installed, and the stream never stops between talks.
    </p>

    <div class="listen-grid">
      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>Right here</h3>
        <p>
          Tap the round play button above. This page works in any modern
          browser, on a computer or a phone, and shows the current talk as it
          plays. Nothing is downloaded and nothing is stored on your device.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>On a phone or tablet</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>iPhone or iPad:</strong> open this page in Safari and tap play. If a friend sent the link inside Instagram, Facebook or WhatsApp, choose <em>Open in Safari</em> from that app&rsquo;s menu first.</li>
          <li><strong>Android:</strong> open this page in Chrome and tap play.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep it at hand:</strong> add the page to your home screen. In Safari tap <em>Share</em>, then <em>Add to Home Screen</em>; in Chrome tap the menu, then <em>Add to Home screen</em>. It then opens with one tap, like an app.</li>
          <li><strong>While listening:</strong> you can lock the screen or switch apps and the sound continues; closing the tab stops it.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>In a radio app</h3>
        <p>
          Any player that accepts a custom station can play the stream. Paste
          this address into VLC, MyTuner Radio, or a similar app:
        </p>
        <code>https://radio.jaikhyapaparampara.com/<wbr>listen/<wbr>guru_vani_radio/<wbr>radio.mp3</code>
        <p>
          On a computer you can also open the
          <a href="https://radio.jaikhyapaparampara.com/public/guru_vani_radio/playlist.m3u">M3U playlist</a>
          in VLC, foobar2000 or Apple Music.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>On an Amazon Echo</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Easiest today &mdash; Bluetooth.</strong> Pair your phone with
          the Echo once, then play this page and the sound comes out of the
          Echo.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Start the stream on your phone.</li>
          <li>Say <em>&ldquo;Alexa, pair&rdquo;</em> &mdash; or open the Alexa app, choose your Echo, then <em>Bluetooth Connections</em> and <em>Pair a New Device</em> &mdash; and select the Echo on your phone.</li>
          <li>Next time, say <em>&ldquo;Alexa, connect to my phone.&rdquo;</em></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>By voice, not yet.</strong> Alexa cannot open a bare stream
          address on its own. &ldquo;Alexa, play Guru Vani Radio&rdquo; needs the
          station listed in a directory such as TuneIn or myTuner Radio, or a
          custom Alexa skill. The station is not listed yet; the
          <a href="/radio/developers/#alexa">developer notes</a> describe those
          options.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Echo Show:</strong> say <em>&ldquo;Alexa, open Silk&rdquo;</em>
          and open the full player &mdash; though playback stops when the screen
          sleeps.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>On a computer</h3>
        <p>
          Keep this page open in a browser tab, or add the stream to the player
          you already use &mdash; VLC, foobar2000, iTunes or Apple Music all
          accept the address above. The
          <a href="https://radio.jaikhyapaparampara.com/public/guru_vani_radio" rel="noopener">full player</a>
          adds request history and a light or dark theme.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="listen-card">
        <h3>In the car</h3>
        <p>
          Play from your phone over Bluetooth, or use Android Auto or CarPlay
          with a radio app that accepts a custom station. The stream is only 64
          kbps, so an hour of listening uses roughly 30&nbsp;MB of mobile data
          &mdash; keep that in mind away from Wi-Fi.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="listen-contact">
      Still stuck, or a question about the station? Write to
      <a href="mailto:kaliputraashish@gmail.com">kaliputraashish@gmail.com</a>
      &mdash; it reaches a real person.
    </p>

    <details class="listen-help">
      <summary>No sound? Try this</summary>
      <ol>
        <li>Tap the play button once more. iPhone, iPad and most Android browsers require a tap before sound may start &mdash; this is a browser rule, not a fault in the station. On iPhone there is no setting that makes it automatic.</li>
        <li>Give it 5&ndash;10 seconds. The stream needs a moment to buffer, especially on mobile data.</li>
        <li>Turn the media volume up with the side buttons, and open Control Center to check the sound is not going to Bluetooth headphones or AirPods.</li>
        <li>If the link opened inside another app (Instagram, Facebook, WhatsApp, Gmail), choose <em>Open in Safari</em> or <em>Open in Chrome</em>, then tap play.</li>
        <li>Close other tabs or apps that are playing audio. A phone call or another player pauses the radio &mdash; tap play again afterwards.</li>
        <li>Once it is playing you can lock the screen and keep listening; closing the tab stops the stream.</li>
        <li>Still silent? The station may be briefly off the air. Try again in a few minutes, or <a href="mailto:kaliputraashish@gmail.com">write to us</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="radio-section radio-dev-cta" aria-labelledby="radio-dev-heading">
    <div class="dev-callout">
      <p class="dev-callout-eyebrow">For app developers &amp; site owners</p>
